#!/usr/bin/env ady2nim
#ady2nim-args js
"""svg_drag -- drag the shapes of an SVG drawing with the mouse, in the browser (ady2nim js).

For a page drawn on the server and kept up to date by htmx: everything else is declared in
the HTML, and only what must follow the mouse every frame runs in the browser. This module
holds what every such drag has in common; the app says what a drag means:

    from svg_drag import Spot, draggable, ...
    def moved(node: Node, centre: Spot, wanted: Spot) -> Spot: ...   # during the drag
    def dropped(node: Node, at: Spot): ...                           # when it is let go
    draggable("g.node", "svg.graph", 580.0, moved, dropped, no_click)

A shape that can be dragged is an element (`g.node` above) carrying its centre, in the
drawing's units, in the attributes data_x and data_y, as the server drew it. While it is
dragged, `moved` is told where the mouse has taken that centre, and answers where the shape
is (it may keep it inside the drawing, or not move it at all). When it is let go, `dropped`
is told where it ended; it usually fills in a hidden form and clicks its button, so that
htmx sends the new place to the server, which draws the page again.

A press that does not move is a click, and goes on to htmx. The click the browser sends
after a drag is swallowed, so a shape is not clicked by being moved. Other clicks go to
`on_click` first (a shift-click, say), which may stop them.

Used by EXAMPLES/DIJKSTRA_HTMX and TOOLS/ACCOUNTING.

---- A few browser words, for a reader who does not write front ends ----

The DOM ("document object model") is the page as the browser holds it: a tree of elements,
one per HTML tag. `document` is its root. Each element is a `Node` here, and the same calls
work on all of them:
  querySelector("css")     the first element below that matches a CSS selector, or nil
                           ("g.node" = a <g> of class node, "#move" = the element with
                           id="move", "input[name=pos]" = the <input> named pos,
                           "g[data_from]" = a <g> that has a data_from attribute)
  querySelectorAll("css")  all of them
  closest("css")           this element or the nearest one above it that matches
  getAttribute / setAttribute   read or write one attribute, as text
The drawing is SVG, which is HTML for pictures: a <g> groups shapes, a <path d="M x1 y1
L x2 y2"> is a line from (x1, y1) to (x2, y2), and transform="translate(dx,dy)" shifts a
group without changing what is in it.

The browser calls a function of ours, a "listener", for each event it is given (see
draggable). An event is a value describing what happened: for the mouse, which button,
whether shift was held, and where (clientX, clientY, in pixels from the window's top left).
A click is three events in this order: mousedown, mouseup, click. Every event goes down the
tree to the element it is for and then back up; htmx listens at the top, on the way back up.
cstring is the browser's own string type: Nim strings are converted at each call.
"""
from dom nimport Event, Element, InputElement, MouseEvent, BoundingRect, Node, document, getBoundingClientRect, addEventListener, getAttribute, setAttribute, querySelector, querySelectorAll, click, stopPropagation, preventDefault, closest, value

# ---- Two units: the mouse's and the drawing's ----
#
# The mouse reports pixels; the server keeps a shape's place in the drawing's own units (its
# viewBox), whatever size the drawing has on the screen. `distinct` makes them two types, so
# adding a pixel to a drawing unit does not compile. The only way across is the scale.

type Pixel_T is distinct float          # on the screen, as the mouse reports it
type Unit_T is distinct float           # in the drawing, as the server keeps a shape's place
type Scale_T is Unit_T / Pixel_T        # drawing units a pixel: pixels * scale = units

type Screen is tuple:                   # a place on the screen
    x: Pixel_T
    y: Pixel_T

type Spot is tuple:                     # a place in the drawing
    x: Unit_T
    y: Unit_T

const MOVED: Pixel_T = 4.0              # how far a press goes before it is a drag

# What the app says a drag means (see the docstring).
type Moved_T is (Node, Spot, Spot) -> Spot   # the shape, its centre when pressed, where the mouse has taken it -> where it is
type Dropped_T is (Node, Spot) -> None       # the shape, where it was let go
type Clicked_T is (Event) -> None            # any click that is not the end of a drag

# ---- The drag, as a small state machine ----
#
#            mousedown on a shape           mousemove more than 4 px
#   IDLE  ------------------------>  PRESSED  ---------------------->  DRAGGING
#    ^  ^                               |                                 |
#    |  +------- mouseup (a click) -----+                                 | mouseup: dropped(..)
#    |                                                                    |
#    +------------- click (the browser's, swallowed) ---- DROPPED  <-----+
#
# IDLE: nothing pressed. PRESSED: a shape is held but has not moved, so letting go is a
# click. DRAGGING: it has moved. DROPPED: a drag has just ended, and the click the browser
# sends after it is not a click.
type Phase_T is enum IDLE, PRESSED, DRAGGING, DROPPED

# Everything the drag knows, in one value. It is a variant record: which fields exist depends
# on the phase. While PRESSED or DRAGGING it holds a shape; while IDLE or DROPPED it has no
# fields at all, so reading `grip.node` then is an error (Nim raises), not a stale value.
#
# A value is built by naming its phase like a function: `PRESSED(node, ...)` fills the fields
# in the order declared below, and `IDLE()` is a grip in phase IDLE, with nothing in it. The
# parentheses matter: bare `IDLE` is the enum member (a Phase_T), `IDLE()` the record (a
# Grip_T), and `grip` is a Grip_T. So `grip = IDLE()` sets the drag back to nothing, and
# `grip.phase == IDLE` asks which phase it is in.
type Grip_T (phase: Phase_T) is record:
    case phase is
        when PRESSED | DRAGGING:
            node:   Node                # the shape held
            down:   Screen              # where the mouse went down
            centre: Spot                # the shape's centre then
            now:    Spot                # and where it is now, as `moved` answered
        when others:
            pass

var grip: Grip_T = IDLE()               # the one drag there is; nothing held at first

# What draggable was given.
var shapes: str = ""                    # the CSS selector of the shapes that can be dragged
var drawing: str = ""                   # the CSS selector of the <svg>
var drawing_width: Unit_T = 0.0               # its width, in its own units
var moved_hook: Moved_T
var dropped_hook: Dropped_T
var clicked_hook: Clicked_T

# ---- Reading the page ----

# The text of attribute ATTR of NODE.
def attr(node: Node, name: str) -> str:
    return str(node.getAttribute(cstring(name)))

# The number in attribute ATTR of NODE ("data_x", "data_y"), as drawing units; 0 if it is not
# a number.
def unit_of(node: Node, name: str) -> Unit_T:
    let r: float | !ParseFailure_T = parse_float(attr(node, name))
    if r is ParseFailure_T:
        return 0.0
    return Unit_T(r)

# Where a shape's centre is, as the server last drew it.
def spot_of(node: Node) -> Spot:
    return Spot(x=unit_of(node, "data_x"), y=unit_of(node, "data_y"))

# Where the mouse was when event M happened.
def screen_of(m: MouseEvent) -> Screen:
    return Screen(x=Pixel_T(float(m.clientX)), y=Pixel_T(float(m.clientY)))

# Whether NODE has the CSS class WORD: class="node from" holds several.
def has_class(node: Node, word: str) -> bool:
    return f" {word} " in " " + attr(node, "class") + " "

# The <input name=NAME> of the page (a hidden field of a form, usually).
def field_named(name: str) -> InputElement:
    return InputElement(document.querySelector(cstring(f"input[name={name}]")))

# How many drawing units a pixel is: the drawing is scaled to fit the page, so this is asked
# each time (the window may have been resized). getBoundingClientRect is the element's size
# and place on the screen, in pixels.
def scale() -> Scale_T:
    let box: BoundingRect = document.querySelector(cstring(drawing)).getBoundingClientRect()
    return drawing_width / Pixel_T(box.width)   # units / pixels: a Scale_T

# ---- Changing the page ----

# Draw NODE, whose centre the server put at CENTRE, at NOW instead: its picture is shifted by
# the difference. Its data_x/data_y stay CENTRE until the server draws the page again.
def shift(node: Node, centre: Spot, now: Spot):
    node.setAttribute(cstring("transform"), cstring(f"translate({now.x - centre.x:.1f},{now.y - centre.y:.1f})"))

# V kept between LO and HI.
def between(v: Unit_T, lo: Unit_T, hi: Unit_T) -> Unit_T:
    return max(lo, min(hi, v))

# For an app with nothing to do on a click.
def no_click(e: Event):
    pass

# ---- The listeners: what each mouse event does, by phase ----

# A button went down. If it is the left one (button 0) and on a shape, hold that shape.
def on_down(e: Event):
    let m: MouseEvent = MouseEvent(e)
    if m.button != 0:
        return
    let node: Node = e.target.closest(cstring(shapes))   # the press may be on a part of the shape
    if node == nil:
        return                          # not on a shape: leave the event to htmx
    let centre: Spot = spot_of(node)
    grip = PRESSED(node, screen_of(m), centre, centre)
    e.preventDefault()                  # stop the browser's own drag, which selects text

# The mouse moved. The browser sends this many times a second, whether a button is down or not.
def on_move(e: Event):
    case grip.phase:
        when PRESSED | DRAGGING:
            let at: Screen = screen_of(MouseEvent(e))
            let dx: Pixel_T = at.x - grip.down.x   # how far from where it went down
            let dy: Pixel_T = at.y - grip.down.y
            if grip.phase == PRESSED and abs(dx) < MOVED and abs(dy) < MOVED:
                return                  # not yet a drag: a hand shakes, and it may still be a click
            let k: Scale_T = scale()    # pixels * scale = drawing units
            let wanted: Spot = Spot(x=grip.centre.x + dx * k, y=grip.centre.y + dy * k)
            let now: Spot = moved_hook(grip.node, grip.centre, wanted)
            grip = DRAGGING(grip.node, grip.down, grip.centre, now)
        when others:
            pass                        # nothing held: an ordinary mouse move

# The button came up.
def on_up(e: Event):
    case grip.phase:
        when PRESSED:
            grip = IDLE()               # it never moved: a click, which the browser sends next and htmx handles
        when DRAGGING:
            # still DRAGGING while the app answers, so a click it makes (on a form's button)
            # goes through on_click
            dropped_hook(grip.node, grip.now)
            grip = DROPPED()
        when others:
            pass

# A click. The browser sends one after every mouseup, a drag's too; htmx would take that one
# as a click on the shape. So after a drag (DROPPED) the click is stopped here, before htmx
# sees it. Any other click is the app's first.
def on_click(e: Event):
    if grip.phase == DROPPED:
        grip = IDLE()
        e.stopPropagation()             # the event goes no further up the tree, so htmx never sees it
        return
    clicked_hook(e)

# Make the shapes SHAPES (a CSS selector) of the drawing DRAWING (the <svg>, WIDE units wide)
# draggable: MOVED, DROPPED and CLICKED say what a drag means (see the docstring).
#
# The listeners are on the whole document, so a drag goes on when the mouse leaves the
# shape. The click listener gets True ("capture"): it is called on the way down the tree,
# before htmx, which listens on the way up.
def draggable(shape_css: str, drawing_css: str, wide: Unit_T, moved: Moved_T, dropped: Dropped_T, clicked: Clicked_T):
    shapes = shape_css
    drawing = drawing_css
    drawing_width = wide
    moved_hook = moved
    dropped_hook = dropped
    clicked_hook = clicked
    document.addEventListener("mousedown", on_down)
    document.addEventListener("mousemove", on_move)
    document.addEventListener("mouseup", on_up)
    document.addEventListener("click", on_click, True)
